Abstract
Developing a facile method to fabricate new heterogeneous Metal-Organic Framework (MOFs) based catalysts with high catalytic activity and stability has drawn significant attention. Herein, we demonstrate a simple in-situ pyrolysis reduction strategy to fabricate a novel three-dimensional (3D) Cu-based catalyst, which displays an outstanding performance for the decomposition of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP). Detailed characterization including SEM, FTIR, XPS, ICP-OES, HRTEM, SAED, XRD and BET confirmed the formation of the $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composites $\mathrm{\left(Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C\right)}$ . Taking advantage of enormous Cu particles in the composite as well as ultrahigh surface area $(196.7\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ of carbon support, $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ presents prominent catalytic activity for the hydrogenation reduction 4-NP to 4-aminophenol (4-AP) with apparent rate constant $(K_{\mathrm{app}})$ of $0.0267\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (the ratio of $K_{\mathrm{app}}$ to the catalyst amount is $\mathbf{\lVert{1}9\ s^{-1}\mathbf{g}^{-1}}$ ), which is dramatically higher than that previous reports. On the contrary, after being washed successively (Cu/C-PC-AW) by FeCl3, HCl aqueous solution and deionized water, the Cu/C porous composite materials exhibit fairly weak catalytic activity. The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ is better than Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathtt{C u O}$ nanoparticles as well as other catalysts in previous reports. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ shows excellent reusability, indicating its potential applications in treatment of water pollution.
Keywords: Three-dimensional $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite, In-situ pyrolysis reduction, Catalysis, 4-nitrophenol
1. Introduction 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) is one of the most common organic pollutants in industrial and agricultural waste water, which can be reduced to 4-aminophenol (4-AP) in the presence of NaBH4. On the contrary, 4-AP is used in the manufacture of many substances such as analgesic and antipyretic drugs, corrosion inhibitors, photographic developers, and so on. [1-4]. At present, great attentions have been focused on the reduction of 4-NP in the presence of NaBH4, which is an effective and environment friendly conversion route by noble metals such as Au, Ag, Pt and Pt–Au nanocatalysts [5-8]. However, the expensive costs restrict their applications seriously. Thus, in order to markedly decrease the cost of metal catalysts, new alternatives with more earth-abundant metals have been developed. Typically, self-assembled composites based on copper and its oxides have been proven to be effective catalysts toward hydrogenation of 4-NP [9-11]. However, there are two urgent issues need to be solved including the complex preparation process of these Cu-based catalysts and the relatively low catalytic activity under mild conditions. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a kind of crystalline solids, which are constructed by inorganic secondary building units and organic linkers. During the past two decades, MOFs have been captured wildly attention because of their diversity of structures and compositions, tunable pore sizes, ultrahigh surface areas, porosity and adjustable surface properties [12-15]. Therefore, MOFs have been widely studied in many application such as drug delivery, gas sorption and separation, heavy metal ion capture, luminescence, batteries and catalysis [16-25]. For example, Zhao et al. successfully fabricated UiO-66 particles, which were coated with nanocomposite shells composed of palladium nanopartic les (Pd NPs) and polypyrrole (PPy), to catalyze the reduction of 4-NP [26]. Meanwhile, researchers have constructed $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@$ ZIF-8 composite materials using a template protection–sacrifice method for the catalytic hydrogenation of 4-nitrophenol [27]. However, these catalysts based on MOFs have low catalytic activity and need a complicated preparation procedure, at the same time, the poor instability of some metal-organic framework based catalysts highly limits their application in catalysis. Therefore, a development in the facile production of catalysts with high catalytic activity and stability based on MOFs is urgently needed.
In the present work, we proposed a facile in-situ high-temperature pyrolysis method to assemble N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite (Cu/C-PC) employing a prepared HKUST-1 MOFs as a precursor. Specifically, Cu/C-PC with remarkable catalytic performance was fabricated by pyrolyzing HKUST-1 at high-temperature under nitrogen gas. Then, Cu/C-PC was further characterized by SEM, HRTEM, SAED, ICP-OES, FTIR, XPS, XRD and BET. The resulting composites consisting of carbon and metal present novel catalytic performance and water stability compared with the parent HKUST-1 on account of the improvement of surface area and graphite degree. The catalytic activities of Cu/C-PC, Cu/C-PC-AW, Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathtt{C u O}$ nanoparticles were evaluated by the reduction efficiency of 4-NP. To the best of our knowledge, Cu/C-PC before washing, which is 3D carbon-based porous structure dispersed internally and outwardly copper nanoparticles, has the better catalytic performance for hydrogenation of 4-NP compared with Cu/C-PC-AW, Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , CuO nanoparticles and various MOF-based catalysts previously reported [28, 29]. Factors relevant to catalytic efficiency such as the content and absorption capacity of Cu/C-PC were considered. Furthermore, the catalytic stability of $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ -PC was also systematically studied in detail.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All the reagents were purchased in commerce and used without further purification:
1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (H3BTC, $98\%$ , Aladdin), copper(II) nitrate trihydrate $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.), ethanol ( $99.7\%$ Chron Chemicals), dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ , Chron Chemicals), 4-nitrophenol (4-NP, C6H5NO3, $99\%$ , Aladdin), sodium borohydride $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ , $98\%$ , Guangdong GuanghuaSci-Tech Co., Ltd.), 4-aminophenol(4-AP, $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{7}N O}$ , Aladdin, $98\%$ ).
## 2.2. Synthesis of HKUST-1
HKUST-1 was synthesized by a simple hydrothermal method [30, 31]. $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (5 g) was dissolved in Milli-Q water $\mathrm{60~mL}$ ) and ethanol $\mathrm{(60~mL)}$ , and then $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ BTC $(1.36~\mathrm{g})$ ) was added in the above solution to form a homogeneous solution. After ultrasonication for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , DMF (4 mL) was slowly added with stirring at room temperature and the mixture was poured into a flask. Thereafter, the flask was immersed in a water bath at $80~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the resultant blue crystal powder was washed with ethanol for several times and dried under vacuum for 10 h.
## 2.3. Preparation of N-doped $\mathbf{Cu/C}$ porous composite
The thermal processing of N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite $\mathrm{\left(Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C\right)}$ was conducted by the following procedures. HKUST-1 was placed in a tube furnace and the equipment was filled gently with ${\bf N}_{2}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the equipment was heated to $500~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ with a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{{^{o}C}~m i n^{-1}}$ and continuous ${\bf N}_{2}$ filling. After kept at $500^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for 2h, the tube furnace was cooled down to $300^{\mathrm{~o}}\mathrm{C}$ with a cooling rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{o}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ and then to room temperature naturally. Finally, the russet N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ was obtained for further catalytic experiment. Compared with Cu/C-PC,
Cu/C-PC materials were washed (Cu/C-PC-AW) successively by FeCl3 aqueous solution, $10\%$ HCl aqueous solution and deionized water.
## 2.4. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
Typically, Cu/C-PC with different amounts ( $\mathrm{1.5~mg}$ , $0.5~\mathrm{mg}$ and $0.2{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) was added in the standard quartz cuvette. 4-NP (0.125 mM, $2.5\mathrm{mL}$ ) aqueous solution and NaBH4 (1.0 M, 0.1 mL) solution were mixed together for the incubation of $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ and then added softly in the standard quartz cuvette. During the reaction, the optical absorbance of the solution was measured by UV–vis spectrophotometer. All the catalytic reactions were conducted at room temperature.
## 2.5. Characterization
Characterization of the crystals was conducted by several different techniques. The morphology of the catalysts and the size of the Cu particles were inspected by scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-4800, JEOL). The content of metal in materials was measured by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, Agilent5110). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) were taken on an FEI Tecnai G2 F20 microscope. X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern was used to analyze the crystallographic information of the samples by using a powder diffractometer (Bruker D8 Advanced Diffractometer System) with a Cu Ka (1.5418 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) source. Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were investigated by a spectrometer with potassium bromide pellets (FTIR, VERTEX 70v, Bruker) in the range of $400{\-}4000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Specific surface areas were calculated by using $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ physisorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ (ASAP2460, MICROMERITICS) and the pore size distribution results were obtained according to the desorption branch of isotherm. Chemical composition information of the crystals was obtained by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS,
Shimadzu); General and high-resolution spectra corresponding to C 1s and $\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ were also determined after light argon ion sputtering. The absorption spectra of different samples were recorded on an UV-Vis-NIR spectrometer (UV-vis 2550, Shimadzu).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Assembly and application of as-prepared catalyst
## <Scheme 1>
The synthetic strategy of N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite (Cu/C-PC) was presented in Scheme
1. The 3D carbon-based porous structure where copper nanoparticles dispersed internally and outwardly was synthesized using a facile high-temperature pyrolysis method with HKUST-1 as template. During the preparation process, the vacuum drying of HKUST-1, namely the activation of materials, can facilitate small solvent molecules and residues to leave from the pores. Meanwhile, the carbonization of MOF also promotes the separation of organic molecules in the framework to form abundant mesoporous and macropores, and leads to the reduction of copper ions in order to form copper and its oxide nanoparticles. Surprisingly, the products present prominent catalytic performance for the hydrogenation of 4-NP. By contrast, little catalytic effect can be seen for the Cu/C-PC-AW.
## 3.2. Characterization of catalyst
## <Fig. 1>
The morphologies of HKUST-1, N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite $\mathrm{\Cu/C\cdotPC)}$ and N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composite after being washed (Cu/C-PC-AW) were investigated by SEM. As shown in Fig. 1a and 1b, Cu-based HKUST-1 is a regular polyhedral crystal with glossy surface. The polyhedral geometry of the original MOF can be retained (Fig. 1c) after being pyrolyzed under ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which is consistent with the report by Wang et al [31]. Nevertheless, the carbonized HKUST-1 lose crystallinity and the Cu particles with a diameter of about $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ are well dispersed on the inside and surface of carbon-based porous skeleton (Fig. 1d), implying the effective reduction of metal centers and the successful pyrolyzation of organic molecules in MOF.
## <Fig. 2>
The morphology of $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ is also consistent with the cross-section morphology, which both contains internal and surface morphologies (Fig. 2). Finally, there is only 3D carbon-based porous structure without Cu particles (Fig. 1f) after being washed and the polyhedral geometry is irregular (Fig. 1e) because of the damage during washing process. The uneven hollow structure of Cu/C-PC-AW is more obvious without copper particles’ coverage. We can see that the HKUST-1 powder presents blue while Cu/C-PC is brownish black according to the photo images of two products (Fig. S1).
## <Fig. 3>
The EDS analyses of the synthesized HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ before and after being washed (Fig. S2, Table S1) have been made. It is glaringly obvious that carbon element is abundant in the three materials which further illustrates that the materials are supported by carbon skeleton. The oxygen content reduced greatly while the nitrogen contents reduced slightly in the MOF precursor after carbonization. The distribution of C, O, N and Cu element of HKUST-1 and Cu/C-PC can be observed from the SEM mappings (Fig. S3, Fig. 3). On the contrary, there is little Cu element in Cu/C-PC-AW compared with the former two materials, indicating that copper metal nanoparticles and its oxide have been successfully washed away (Fig. S4). ICP analysis indicated the loading of Cu element in Cu/C-PC (49.1 wt $\%$ ) was higher than MOF precursor (15.3 wt $\%$ ), and there was
no copper in Cu/C-PC-AW.
## <Fig. 4>
HRTEM was further employed to confirm the formation of these copper and its oxide nanoparticles in carbon skeleton. Fig. 4a clearly shows nanoparticles in the C skeleton. It can be observed from Fig. 4b that the interplanar distances of the selected area are calculated to be 0.211 nm and $0.246\mathrm{nm}$ , attributable to the (111) lattice planes of Cu and the (111) lattice planes of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ respectively. Meanwhile, as shown in Fig. 4c, the polycrystalline structures of $\mathrm{cu}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were observed from the pronounced rings.
The XRD pattern of Cu/C-PC (Fig. 4d) presented the characteristic peaks at $43^{\circ}$ , $50.1^{\circ}$ and $74^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (111), (220) and (220) crystal planes of Cu (JCPDS 04-0836), respectively. The peak at $36.4^{\circ}$ is assigned to the (111) planes of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (JCPDS 78-2076). These results are well in accordance with the pronounced rings in SAED pattern. Based on previous studies, the relative content (wt $\%$ ) of the Cu and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}$ phases can be calculated by the heights of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the Cu (111) and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (111) planes [32-34]. Therefore, the relative contents of Cu and Cu2O in Cu/C-PC were $\sim72.6$ and ${\sim}27.4\mathrm{wt}\%$ according to the ratios of $\mathrm{{Icu(111)}}/(\mathrm{{Icu(111)}}$ + ICu2O (111)) and $\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{Cu2O(111)}}/(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{Cu(111)}}+\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{Cu2O(111)}}).$ .
The formation mechanism of Cu (0) and $\mathrm{Cu}$ (I) after heating of HKUST-1 in furnace can be attributed to the fact that the precursor HKUST-1 lost its lattice water during thermolysis. Moreover, the BTC organic ligands were decomposed into carbon and various gases including hydrogen, carbon monoxide, and benzene, as well as other fragments from the incomplete decomposed ligands, which have predominant reducibility and might reduce Cu (II) to Cu (I) and Cu (0) [35, 36]. Furthermore, the unique 3D nature of HKUST-1 can facilitate the growth of the particles during the calcining process. The reduction potential of $\mathtt{C u}$ (II) is larger than -0.27 V, so that HKUST-1 would form metal nanoparticles after calcination in ${\bf N}_{2}$ flows on the basis of previous report [37].
## <Fig. 5>
## <Fig. 6>
The chemical composition analysis of the three materials was conducted by using X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS), and the typical survey spectra clearly indicate the existence of carbon, oxygen and copper in HKUST-1 and Cu/C-PC. As shown in Fig. 6, Cu/C-PC-AW consists of carbon and oxygen, and C 1s and O 1s peaks can be observed at 283.4 and $531.5\mathrm{eV}$ in Fig. 5a, respectively. Compared with the oxygen content of original HKUST-1 samples, the oxygen content in HKUST-1 annealed in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are lower than that of HKUST-1 because of the decomposition of small molecules during calcination, which is consistent with those observed in EDS data (Table S1). Meanwhile, the chemical states of carbon and copper in HKUST-1 changed greatly before and after calcination. In Cu/C-PC (Fig. 6b), five peaks (284.6, 285.5, 286.6, 287.7 and 289.1 eV) observed from C 1s spectrum are attributed to C−C/C−H, C−N, C−O, $C{=}0$ , and O−C=O, respectively. Copper has three chemical states (Fig. 6e). It is worth noting that the binding energies at 931.1, 124.4 and $76.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ in HKUST-1 and Cu/C-PC are solely assigned to Cu 2p, Cu 3s and Cu 3p, respectively, which disappear after being washed. This result is in keeping with the SEM results (Fig. 1). In addition, the C 1s XPS spectra of Cu/C-PC (Fig. 6b) and Cu/C-PC-AW (Fig. 6c) are uniform, which further confirms the conservation of carbon skeleton after being washed. It is worth noting that the N 1s spectrum of Cu/C-PC can be deconvoluted into two types including pyridinic-N (398.7eV) and pyrrolic-N $400.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , as shown in Fig. S5 [38,
39]. During the preparation of HKUST-1, $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}\cdot3\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and DMF could provide Nitrogen species. At the same time, EDS verified the existence of the N element in HKUST-1, as shown as Fig. S2 and Table S1. Therefore, the mechanism for the doping of N could be concluded that HKUST-1 can give some N-dopant species in the carbon materials during the pyrolysis process. Moreover, because of the loss of unstable N at high temperature, the content of N in materials was decreased after pyrolysis.
The functional groups on the surface of three materials were studied by using an FTIR spectrophotometer (Fig. 5b). The peak at $628~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is assigned to the Cu–O stretching vibration and its broadening illuminates the presence of mixed phases in the prepared system, which is consistent with the results examined by XPS [40]. Furthermore, the asymmetric stretching and symmetric stretching of carboxylate groups $(-\mathrm{COO-})$ in BDC appears at $1388\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1630\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The band at 3441cm−1 corresponds to the characteristic vibrations of $\mathrm{O-H}$ bonds. However, It is a remarkable fact that the peak at $721~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{C-H}$ vibration in 1,3,5-trisubstituted benzene ring disappears after pyrolysis, indicating the successful decomposition of organic ligand. Therefore, the IR spectrum indicates the successful synthesis of HKUST-1 and the pyrolysis of organic molecules during the calcination process.
To further research the porosity of the material observed by SEM, Cu/C-PC was measured through a Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) method. As shown in Fig.5c, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption /desorption isotherms disclose a type-IV isotherm which has an obvious hysteresis loop at the relative pressure $\left(\mathrm{{p/p_{0}}}\right)$ of $0.4{\sim}0.5$ with a total pore volume of $0.22\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$ corresponding to BET surface area with $196.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ The pore-size distribution of Cu/C-PC (Fig.5d) possesses both micropores and mesopores in a certain scope, which can be demonstrated by the hysteresis loop. Hence, the
Cu/C-PC is a good candidate for catalysis, which can provide plenty of spaces and active sites for catalytic reaction.
## 3.3 Performance of Cu/C-PC for the reduction of 4-NP
## <Fig. 7>
As the most common organic pollutant, 4-NP often appears in industrial and agricultural waste water. The reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP has been widely conducted using various catalysts [41-45]. In this work, we choose the catalytic reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP by NaBH4 as a bench mark reaction to evaluate the catalytic activities of Cu/C-PC. After the addition of NaBH4 solution, the UV-vis absorbance band of 4-NP solution red shifted from 317.0 to $401.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 7b) with the color change from pale yellow to bright yellow (Fig. 7a), which demonstrates the deprotonation of 4-NP. The absorption of this peak almost remained unchanged after 60 minutes (Fig. 7c), implying that the hydrogenation reaction could not happen in the absence of catalysts, even with a large excess of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . However, the reduction of 4-NP initiated immediately after the addition of the N-doped Cu/C porous composites while the absorption peak at $401\ \mathrm{nm}$ decreased rapidly and eventually disappeared during three minutes along with a concomitant increase of a new absorption peak at $305~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating the formation of 4-AP (Fig. 7d). When $1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ and $0.5~\mathrm{mg}$ catalysts were added, the complete reduction of 4-NP completed within 60 s (Fig. S6). When the usage mass of the N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composites was reduced to $0.2\ \mathrm{\mg}$ , the reaction proceeded with a conversion over $99\%$ in 150 s (as shown in Fig. 7d). Simultaneously, the color of the reaction solution changes from light yellow to colorless which represents the complete reduction of 4-NP (Fig. 7a).
In this catalytic reaction system, the initial concentration of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ solution can be considered as a constant throughout the whole reduction process with respect to the concentration of 4-NP in the presence of a large excess of NaBH4. Therefore, the reduction rate is assumed to be independent of the NaBH4 concentration, and a pseudo first-order kinetic equation can be applied to evaluate the catalytic rate. A good linear correlation between $\ln(\mathrm{C_{t}/C_{0})}$ and time was display in Fig. 7e ( $\mathrm{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{t}}}^{}$ is the concentration of 4-NP at the reaction time t, $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ is the initial concentration, and the ratio of $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ is reckoned according to the relative intensity of the respective absorbances, $\mathrm{{A}_{\mathrm{{t}}}/\mathrm{{A}_{0}.)}}$ , confirming that the reduction of 4-NP followed the pseudo-first-order kinetics mentioned above, and the half life $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right)$ and the apparent rate constant $(K_{\mathrm{app}})$ were calculated as $22.65\mathrm{~s~}$ and $0.0267\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ , respectively. Moreover, the ratio of $K_{\mathrm{app}}$ to the catalyst amount $(\bf K=K_{a p p}\ m^{-1}.$ ) calculated is $119\ \mathrm{s^{-1}g^{-1}}$ , which is higher than other reported non-noble metal catalysts such as Cu cubes, Cu polyhedrons, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@$ ZIF-8, Cu@CMK-8 and Ni/SNTs [10, 27, 46, 47]. Besides, $\mathrm{{Cu/C{\cdot}P C}}$ also displays prominent catalytic performance even when it is in comparison with various noble metal catalysts, such as CF-AuNPs, Au sponge and Au/Cu Nanoclusters [42, 48, 49] (Table S2).
It is well known that an ideal catalyst should fulfill the demands of high catalytic activity and easy desorption for reactant. The absorption capacity of the N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composites for the equivalent amount of 4-AP was studied for comparison and calculated as $6.56~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ at $60\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. S7). This result demonstrates that the catalyst exhibits very little binding for 4-AP, which indicates that $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ has only catalysis. The catalytic kinetic experiments and the absorption kinetic experiments indicate high catalytic activity and low absorption capacity of product so that the excellent catalytic reduction of 4-NP by Cu/C-PC can be further confirmed.
## <Fig. 8>
In addition, the catalytic cycle test was performed in order to study the recyclability and stability. In each cycle, the catalysts were carefully separated from the solution by centrifugation and then dried for the next cycle of catalysis. As shown in Fig. 8a, till the fifth cycle, the hydrogenation of 4-NP can be completed in 3 minutes, indicating that the catalytic sites of the N-doped $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ porous composites are extremely stable. The half life $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right)$ and the apparent rate constant $(\mathrm{k_{app}})$ were calculated respectively as 28.67 s and 0.0211 $\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ , which is litt le slower than the first-order reaction (Fig. 8b). This outstanding recyclability of Cu/C-PC may be attributed to the protective effect from 3D porous carbon skeleton.
To further substantiate the role of copper metal nanoparticles and its oxide in the catalysis reaction, the catalytic performances of Cu/C-PC-AW, Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathtt{C u O}$ were conducted (Fig. S8 and Fig. S9). The experimental results indicated that the catalytic effect of $\mathrm{Cu/C}$ -PC-AW was negligible compared with Cu/C-PC. Besides, the catalytic performances of Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and CuO nanoparticles were lower than that of Cu/C-PC. Noteworthily, the catalytic performance of Cu is more outstanding than corresponding oxide. The content of Cu in $\mathrm{Cu/C\mathrm{-}P C}$ is about 2.6 times that of cuprous oxide according to the Fig. 4. The above results indicate that $\mathrm{Cu}$ in Cu/C-PC catalyst has outstanding contributions to the reduction of 4-NP, and the synergistic effects between active Cu species and carbon skeleton enhance the catalytic activity of catalyst.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a facile in-situ pyrolysis reduction route to prepare a new catalyst based on HKUST-1, which exhibits remarkable catalytic performance and excellent cycle stability in the hydrogenation of 4-NP. The conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP was over $99\%$ within 150 s and the apparent rate constant $\mathrm{\bf{K}}_{\mathrm{{app}}},$ ) was calculated as $0.0267~\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ . Moreover, Cu/C-PC demonstrated the best catalytic performance compared with Cu/C-PC-AW, Cu, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and CuO nanoparticles and better than previous reports in Table S2. Therefore, we conclude that the copper metal particles have the largest contribution to catalytic testing and the synergistic effects between active Cu species and carbon skeleton can enhance the catalytic activity of catalyst. This study may provide a new recognition of fabricating MOF-derived non noble metal carbon-based catalysts, which would have high potential in diverse catalytic reactions and treatment of environmental pollution. In the future, we will use this method to prepare other supported metal oxide catalysts and explore their potential.
